React Hooks are one of the most important concepts to understand because they are how React components manage:

Hooks are special React functions that allow functional components
to use React features such as state, lifecycle behavior, refs, context, and reusable logic.
Hooks let React track component state and rerender components when that state changes.

State
Side effects
API calls
DOM access
Reusable logic

~~~~~~~~~



function UsersPage() {
  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    async function loadUsers() {
      const response =
        await fetch(
          "http://localhost:3001/users"
        );

      const data =
        await response.json();

      setUsers(data);
    }

    loadUsers();
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      {users.map(user => (
        <p key={user.id}>
          {user.name}
        </p>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

how it works:

Page loads
     ↓
useEffect runs
     ↓
fn call : loadUsers then Call NestJS API
     ↓
Get data
     ↓
setUsers
     ↓
Rerender
     ↓
Display users




~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~useRouter()
useRouter() is one of the most commonly used hooks in Next.js
: A hook that allows you to navigate between pages programmatically from JavaScript code.


useRouter() is a Next.js client-side hook from next/navigation that allows programmatic navigation. It provides methods such as:

push() → navigate to a new route
replace() → navigate without adding history
back() → go back
forward() → go forward
refresh() → re-fetch server-rendered data




'use client'
import { useRouter } from 'next/navigation';

export default function LoginPage() {
  const router = useRouter();

  return (
    <button
      onClick={() => router.push('/dashboard')}
    >
      Login
    </button>
  );
}

Login
  ↓
router.push('/dashboard')
  ↓
Navigate
  ↓
/dashboard





