2. useEffect
: Run code when something happens.

Examples:
Component loads
State changes
Props change


function Users() {
  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch("/api/users")
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(data => setUsers(data));
  }, []);

  return <div>{users.length}</div>;
}

what happens :

1. Component renders
   users = []

2. useEffect runs
   API call starts

3. API response comes

4. setUsers(data)

5. Component re-renders
   users = actual data



useState = stores data

setState = triggers re-render

useEffect = runs side effects after render

useEffect + setState = causes another render




~~~~~~~Dependency Array
Run once:
useEffect(() => {}, []);

Run when teamId changes:
useEffect(() => {}, [teamId]);

Run every render:
useEffect(() => {});




3. useRef
:lets you store a value that:

Persists between renders
Does not cause a re-render when changed


function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const countRef = useRef(0);

  return (
    <>
      <p>State: {count}</p>
      <p>Ref: {countRef.current}</p>

      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        State++
      </button>

      <button onClick={() => countRef.current++}>
        Ref++
      </button>
    </>
  );
}

When you click Ref++:
countRef.current changes
↓
No re-render
↓
Screen still shows old value

When you click State++:
State changes
↓
Re-render
↓
Now latest ref value is also visible

Real Project Use Cases:
